    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 161148\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n348 AM PST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Precipitation and cooler weather arrives Monday and lasts\n  through Tuesday\n\n* A colder system arrives in the mid-week, causing Winter Storm\n  Watch for the Eastern Sierra for accumulating snow and bringing \n  another batch of scattered showers for the rest of the region.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Sunday\n\nA cold front is currently making its way south along the Sierra  \nNevada and is expected to arrive to the Eastern Sierra this \nafternoon. Most of the moisture will be focused on areas to the \nnorth and west, but the trough associated with this system should \nhave enough of a moisture feed to offer light snowfall along the \nhighest peaks of Inyo county. The trough does manage to pull \nadditional moisture from the south after it clears the Eastern \nSierra and moves into southern Nevada, offering better chances for\nlight showers and breezy to gusty winds. Models hint a some \nunorganized prefrontal showers in the desert valleys in the late \nafternoon and early evening, with more organized lines of showers \narriving along the front into Monday night. The strongest winds \nwill be along the leading edge of the front, and arrive slightly \nbefore the more organized showers. Rain chances peak late Monday \nnight and into Tuesday morning then push east out of the region \nTuesday evening. Rainfall totals haven't changed much over the \nlast few forecast updates with around trace amounts to 0.20\" for \nall but Northeast Clark Co, Mohave Co, and Lincoln Co which will \nrange from 0.25 to 0.60\". This will be over a fair amount of time \nmeaning the flood risks will be on the lower side.\n\nAfter a brief pause in the mid week, precip returns early\nWednesday as another trough builds into the region. This one \nwill have a stronger tap of moisture as it passes through the \nSierra Nevada, offering the potential for much more fresh snow in \nthe Eastern Sierra. The current forecast calls for 10 to 18 inches\nbetween 5,000 and 8,000 feet and 18 to 36 above 8,000 with \nlocally higher totals along the ridgeline. Winds will be strong as\nthis snow falls, above 50 mph along the highest peaks. While the \nmoisture will be focused on the Sierra, some will break into the \nrest of the CWA with lighter accumulations of snow for higher \nelevations in southern Nevada (possibly 4 inches at Mt Charleston)\nalong with another batch of rain showers that could linger into \nearly Friday. Most rain rates look to stay light, but will add up \nover time with most of the region setting up for 0.10 to 0.40 \ninches of rain as of the current forecast. The timing of all of \nthis is subject to change as this trough is currently located over\nthe Gulf of Alaska and models are starting to hint at it slowing \nas it moves inland at the California and Oregon border. So this \nmay be something to keep checking back in on as the forecast \nupdates. \n\nBeyond Friday, a quick ridge sets up over the region and prevents \nadditional rains from pushing this far south and starting a weak \nwarming trend through the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Light,\nsomewhat variable winds will continue through this evening. \nCeilings will gradually degrade through the forecast period, \nbecoming SCT-BKN between 5-7kft by mid-morning, decreasing to \n3-5kft late evening and overnight as scattered rain showers move \ninto the Las Vegas Valley. Brief visibility reductions to 5-6SM \nwill also be possible with passing showers, and ceilings below \n2kft cannot be ruled out early Tuesday, with a 30% chance for \nthese lower ceilings to occur. Overnight, southerly winds increase\nto 8-10KT, then shift to the southwest and become elevated/gusty \nTuesday morning, with gusts to around 25KT possible as showers \nmove east. Ceilings gradually improve near and beyond the end of \nthe forecast period.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds across the\nregion will be somewhat variable through the forecast period, \nwith speeds generally around 5-10KT. The primary exception will be\nKBIH in the northern Owens Valley, where up-valley winds gusting \nto around 25KT are expected later this morning through around \nsunset. The primary aviation concern will be decreasing ceilings, \nexpected to drop from around 7-10kft this morning to 4-6kft this \nevening and tonight as low elevation rain and mountain snow moves \nacross the region. Lower ceilings are possible with passing \nshowers, along with brief periods of reduced visibility if any \nshowers move over area terminals. This is especially true for \nKBIH, where ceilings have a 40-50% chance to drop below 2kft late \nin the period.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
    "risk": "MEDIUM",
